  "id": "COMPOUND:2248 - PROTEIN:6319",
  "verbalization": "Myricetin, a flavonoid, is implicated in the relationship with UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B4 (UGT2B4) through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Myricetin acts as a target for several proteins, including Werner syndrome ATP-dependent helicase, which in turn targets UGT2B4. This indicates a potential mechanism involving the regulation of UGT2B4 by these proteins. Additionally, Myricetin is linked to 5-Hydroxymethyl-9-methoxycanthin-6-one, which also targets UGT2B4, suggesting a broader role in modulating UGT2B4 activity. The presence of multiple targets and their shared downstream effect on UGT2B4 supports a mechanism where Myricetin may influence UGT2B4 through indirect pathways, possibly by affecting the activity or expression of the upstream proteins. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, with Myricetin potentially modulating UGT2B4 through a network of interacting proteins. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
